{"text": " that a lot with just a compilation check with Lean. I didn't say that before. With Lean, you write a proof. So you write propositions, you write a proof, and you combine that with a name into a theorem, and Lean will check it for you automatically. It will check the proof, and if it doesn't compile, then the proof is wrong. If it compiles, the proof is right. So you can basically have AI generate the proof in whatever way. The proof doesn't have to be pretty, it doesn't have to be nice, and you know that the proof is correct, more or less, right? There could be bugs in the Lean, proof checking kernel, and things like that. And then, you can also have the AI reason about the claims, which are the prop type, or the proposition type in Lean. You can have the AI reason about those claims, and I feel like it'll probably do a pretty good job, but that's possibly an area where human expertise is maybe still very valuable. So, but whatever, it doesn't matter. We're heading towards being able to formally verify.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.579999999999997, "end": 3.76, "text": " that a lot with just a compilation check with Lean.", "tokens": [50363, 326, 257, 1256, 351, 655, 257, 23340, 2198, 351, 45661, 13, 50555], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16530860374713766, "compression_ratio": 1.9718875502008033, "no_speech_prob": 0.5994651913642883, "words": [{"word": " that", "start": 0.579999999999997, "end": 0.94, "probability": 0.47361257672309875}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.94, "end": 1.2, "probability": 0.9621328115463257}, {"word": " lot", "start": 1.2, "end": 1.46, "probability": 0.9984645843505859}, {"word": " with", "start": 1.46, "end": 1.92, "probability": 0.8972002863883972}, {"word": " just", "start": 1.92, "end": 2.24, "probability": 0.9926114678382874}, {"word": " a", "start": 2.24, "end": 2.64, "probability": 0.996950089931488}, {"word": " compilation", "start": 2.64, "end": 3.16, "probability": 0.9909886717796326}, {"word": " c